There is an issue opened in github on this project:
"Found a bug in appimage-integrator-downloaded's wait_for_file_copy() function. If the target AppImage file is removed, renamed, or moved (e.g., by a previous successful integration, or if the user manually moves it) while the function is polling, stat fails silently:

bashcurrent_size=$(stat -f%z "$file" 2>/dev/null || stat -c%s "$file" 2>/dev/null)

Both stat variants error out, current_size becomes an empty string, which never equals previous_size, so stable_count never reaches 3 and the while true loop never breaks. There's no timeout or missing-file check, so the process spins on sleep 1 forever — I had two separate instances stuck running for two days straight (visible via execsnoop, each firing several sleep 1 calls per second, holding the appimage-integrator lock file open the entire time).

Repro: Run the integrator on a file, then delete/move that file before the 3-second stability check completes (or before the download actually starts).
"

The fix is straightforward — add a missing-file check inside the loop and break out if the file disappears.

Fix infinite loop by returning early if the file disappears during polling

appimage-integrator-downloaded
Applied
Now handle the return value at the call site so the notify step is skipped if the file is gone:

Handle the case where wait_for_file_copy returns 1 (file disappeared) to avoid notifying about a missing file

appimage-integrator-downloaded
Applied
Two changes made:

[ -f "$file" ] || return 1 — at the top of the loop, before any stat call. If the file is gone, the function exits immediately with a non-zero status instead of spinning forever.

The call site now checks if ! wait_for_file_copy ... and exits cleanly with a warning log if the file disappeared, so the notify step is never reached for a missing file.

